      "result": {
        "items": [
          {
            "source": "postgresql_docs",
            "doc_id": "18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html",
            "title": "13.5. Serialization Failure Handling #",
            "source_url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html",
            "content_version": "2bd25ec106c95468540013c128a809303777950292de29d66e7cdff452da654c",
            "doc_key": "013995f0037a6d31ae147406917508ad3c858ee98ea295b1c17a4f8fcae9a99a",
            "rank": 1,
            "selection_score": 0.6325568739698989,
            "relevance": 0.5969818596926776,
            "history_overlap": 0.0,
            "supplement_score": 0.36800674479742057,
            "reason": "history_comparison",
            "comparison": {
              "status": "possible_supplement",
              "candidate": {
                "source": "postgresql_docs",
                "doc_id": "18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html",
                "title": "13.5. Serialization Failure Handling #",
                "content_version": "2bd25ec106c95468540013c128a809303777950292de29d66e7cdff452da654c",
                "start": 563,
                "end": 769,
                "text": "In some cases it is also appropriate to retry unique-key failures, which have SQLSTATE code 23505 (unique_violation), and exclusion constraint failures, which have SQLSTATE code 23P01 (exclusion_violation)."
              },
              "history": {
                "source": "postgresql_docs",
                "doc_id": "18/transaction-iso.html",
                "title": "13.2. Transaction Isolation #",
                "content_version": "f9acf0c8eca4549c764158ea7a1de39f50e6a2b3c3291b0be604950e164953af",
                "start": 19820,
                "end": 20174,
                "text": "It is important that an environment which uses this technique have a generalized way of handling serialization failures (which always return with an SQLSTATE value of '40001'), because it will be very hard to predict exactly which transactions might contribute to the read/write dependencies and need to be rolled back to prevent serialization anomalies."
              },
              "covered_evidence": [],
              "additional_evidence": [
                {
                  "status": "possible_supplement",
                  "candidate": {
                    "source": "postgresql_docs",
                    "doc_id": "18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html",
                    "title": "13.5. Serialization Failure Handling #",
                    "content_version": "2bd25ec106c95468540013c128a809303777950292de29d66e7cdff452da654c",
                    "start": 563,
                    "end": 769,
                    "text": "In some cases it is also appropriate to retry unique-key failures, which have SQLSTATE code 23505 (unique_violation), and exclusion constraint failures, which have SQLSTATE code 23P01 (exclusion_violation)."
                  },
                  "history": {
                    "source": "postgresql_docs",
                    "doc_id": "18/transaction-iso.html",
                    "title": "13.2. Transaction Isolation #",
                    "content_version": "f9acf0c8eca4549c764158ea7a1de39f50e6a2b3c3291b0be604950e164953af",
                    "start": 19820,
                    "end": 20174,
                    "text": "It is important that an environment which uses this technique have a generalized way of handling serialization failures (which always return with an SQLSTATE value of '40001'), because it will be very hard to predict exactly which transactions might contribute to the read/write dependencies and need to be rolled back to prevent serialization anomalies."
                  },
                  "scores": {
                    "relevance": 0.5969818596926776,
                    "context_similarity": 0.6375743517193047,
                    "cross_encoder_relevance": 0.4802261734935697,
                    "goal_similarity": 0.6470200109208667,
                    "supplement": 0.36800674479742057,
                    "contradiction": 0.0018573494162410498,
                    "entailment": 0.03128223493695259,
                    "neutral": 0.9668604731559753
                  }
                },
                {
                  "status": "possible_supplement",
                  "candidate": {
                    "source": "postgresql_docs",
                    "doc_id": "18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html",
                    "title": "13.5. Serialization Failure Handling #",
                    "content_version": "2bd25ec106c95468540013c128a809303777950292de29d66e7cdff452da654c",
                    "start": 770,
                    "end": 1001,
                    "text": "For example, if the application selects a new value for a primary key column after inspecting the currently stored keys, it could get a unique-key failure because another application instance selected the same new key concurrently."
                  },
                  "history": {
                    "source": "postgresql_docs",
                    "doc_id": "18/transaction-iso.html",
                    "title": "13.2. Transaction Isolation #",
                    "content_version": "f9acf0c8eca4549c764158ea7a1de39f50e6a2b3c3291b0be604950e164953af",
                    "start": 20811,
                    "end": 21029,
                    "text": "In particular, it is possible to see unique constraint violations caused by conflicts with overlapping Serializable transactions even after explicitly checking that the key isn't present before attempting to insert it."
                  },
                  "scores": {
                    "relevance": 0.40291897143598193,
                    "context_similarity": 0.6359409475758048,
                    "cross_encoder_relevance": 0.17402556588920623,
                    "goal_similarity": 0.501016145241743,
                    "supplement": 0.21190549485663537,
                    "contradiction": 0.05542156472802162,
                    "entailment": 0.1175742894411087,
                    "neutral": 0.8270041942596436
                  }
                },
                {
                  "status": "possible_supplement",
                  "candidate": {
                    "source": "postgresql_docs",
                    "doc_id": "18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html",
                    "title": "13.5. Serialization Failure Handling #",
                    "content_version": "2bd25ec106c95468540013c128a809303777950292de29d66e7cdff452da654c",
                    "start": 1002,
                    "end": 1178,
                    "text": "This is effectively a serialization failure, but the server will not detect it as such because it cannot “see” the connection between the inserted value and the previous reads."
                  },
                  "history": {
                    "source": "postgresql_docs",
                    "doc_id": "18/transaction-iso.html",
                    "title": "13.2. Transaction Isolation #",
                    "content_version": "f9acf0c8eca4549c764158ea7a1de39f50e6a2b3c3291b0be604950e164953af",
                    "start": 15186,
                    "end": 15431,
                    "text": "This monitoring does not introduce any blocking beyond that present in repeatable read, but there is some overhead to the monitoring, and detection of the conditions which could cause a serialization anomaly will trigger a serialization failure."
                  },
                  "scores": {
                    "relevance": 0.26389502026232603,
                    "context_similarity": 0.6890378436639903,
                    "cross_encoder_relevance": 0.048980596789491945,
                    "goal_similarity": 0.3560012017506835,
                    "supplement": 0.15670759620022465,
                    "contradiction": 0.10985852777957916,
                    "entailment": 0.028323031961917877,
                    "neutral": 0.8618184328079224
                  }
                }
              ],
              "uncertain_evidence": [
                {
                  "status": "uncertain",
                  "candidate": {
                    "source": "postgresql_docs",
                    "doc_id": "18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html",
                    "title": "13.5. Serialization Failure Handling #",
                    "content_version": "2bd25ec106c95468540013c128a809303777950292de29d66e7cdff452da654c",
                    "start": 168,
                    "end": 299,
                    "text": "As previously stated, applications using these levels must be prepared to retry transactions that fail due to serialization errors."
                  },
                  "history": {
                    "source": "postgresql_docs",
                    "doc_id": "18/transaction-iso.html",
                    "title": "13.2. Transaction Isolation #",
                    "content_version": "f9acf0c8eca4549c764158ea7a1de39f50e6a2b3c3291b0be604950e164953af",
                    "start": 11147,
                    "end": 11246,
                    "text": "Applications using this level must be prepared to retry transactions due to serialization failures."
                  },
                  "scores": {
                    "relevance": 0.4090844683740418,
                    "context_similarity": 0.949971783143949,
                    "cross_encoder_relevance": 0.05014724941292099,
                    "goal_similarity": 0.562914705071665,
                    "supplement": 0.0,
                    "contradiction": 0.0021087585482746363,
                    "entailment": 0.05382225289940834,
                    "neutral": 0.944068968296051
                  }
                },
                {
                  "status": "uncertain",
                  "candidate": {
                    "source": "postgresql_docs",
                    "doc_id": "18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html",
                    "title": "13.5. Serialization Failure Handling #",
                    "content_version": "2bd25ec106c95468540013c128a809303777950292de29d66e7cdff452da654c",
                    "start": 1767,
                    "end": 1889,
                    "text": "Therefore, PostgreSQL does not offer an automatic retry facility, since it cannot do so with any guarantee of correctness."
                  },
                  "history": {
                    "source": "postgresql_docs",
                    "doc_id": "18/transaction-iso.html",
                    "title": "13.2. Transaction Isolation #",
                    "content_version": "f9acf0c8eca4549c764158ea7a1de39f50e6a2b3c3291b0be604950e164953af",
                    "start": 18103,
                    "end": 18224,
                    "text": "Predicate locks in PostgreSQL, like in most other database systems, are based on data actually accessed by a transaction."
                  },
                  "scores": {
                    "relevance": 0.382236236602663,
                    "context_similarity": 0.44504677215734467,
                    "cross_encoder_relevance": 0.016310677461794758,
                    "goal_similarity": 0.5390614762344637,
                    "supplement": 0.0,
                    "contradiction": 0.8058463335037231,
                    "entailment": 0.001395643106661737,
                    "neutral": 0.19275802373886108
                  }
                }
              ],
              "scores": {
                "coverage": 0.0,
                "supplement": 0.36800674479742057
              },
